# Chroma library, modern Earth: THE ODD ONE OUT, part 1 (S4 the odd one out, v22.3, DRAFT cast_want: odd).
# The character has been at a place for a year or more and their own ways are rare there (under about one in seven).
# The Engine fills {place} with the place's plain name. Both pairs are newcomers: new, visible, every slip seen by all.
# Every option carries odd: blend | hold | leave (table1.txt). Chances: per way, its one-letter option plus its cross (first
# letter) = 140; blend 60-90, hold 45-85, leave 70-95. Syntax: header of build.py.
#   pair 1  prot (a crew, a watch, a unit), newcomer      1a WU (s1) / 1b BRG (s2)
#   pair 2  prod (a workshop, a farm, a kitchen), newcomer 2a UB (s3) / 2b WRG (s4)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 1: protection, newcomer

== the new face whose ways stand out on the crew
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: WU
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, safety
horizon: months
roles: regular, keeper, colleague
sphere: prot
ladder: newcomer
cast_want: odd
earth: you are the odd one out at a place you go: on the crew you joined, your ways stand out, and the others have started to notice
times: Someone whose kind makes up less than about one in seven of a group is watched more closely and pressed harder to fit in (tokens: Kanter 1977)
gap: 3-8
scene: A year into {place}, {N} is still the new face, and still the only one there who goes about things this way. At drills {Ns} habits draw looks, and over tea someone does an impression of {N} that gets a big laugh. Afterwards one of the regulars says quietly that the crew works best when everyone works the same way. Nobody is cruel about it, but everyone is watching what {N} does next.
scene W: A crew that keeps people safe has to move as one, and {N} owes it that, but also owes it honesty about what works.
scene U: {N} can see why the crew does things its way, and also where {Ns} own way might be better; the question is which reasons hold up.
scene B: A newcomer who is laughed at gets the dull shifts and no say. {N} weighs what standing out costs now, and what it might buy later.
scene R: The laugh stung more than {N} lets on. Pretending to be someone else at every drill would sting more.
scene G: {N} learned these ways from people at home who did this work for years. They are not just habits; they are where {N} comes from.
- W | learn the crew's drill to the letter: a team that saves lives moves as one | odd: blend | v: conformity, security | chance: 80
- U | keep your own method, and write down why it is quicker, for anyone who asks | odd: hold | v: self-direction, achievement | act: keep their own method, and write down why it is quicker, for anyone who asks | chance: 60
- B | move to a crew where your ways count for something and you can rise | odd: leave | v: achievement, power | act: move to a crew where their ways count for something and they can rise | chance: 75
- R | laugh at the impression with them, and throw yourself into their way of doing it | odd: blend | v: hedonism, stimulation | mark: made a friend | act: laugh at the impression with them, and throw themselves into the crew's way of doing it | chance: 70
- G | keep to the ways the old hands back home taught, quietly, drill after drill | odd: hold | v: tradition | chance: 55
- W>U | ask the keeper for a fair trial of both methods at the next drill | odd: hold | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 60
- U>B | work out which crew nearby would value your training most, and transfer there | odd: leave | v: achievement, self-direction | act: work out which crew nearby would value their training most, and transfer there | chance: 80
- B>R | play it the crew's way for a season, to earn the freedom to differ later | odd: blend | v: power, self-direction | chance: 65
- R>G | stand up at the next meeting and say, warmly, where your ways come from | odd: hold | v: tradition, stimulation | act: stand up at the next meeting and say, warmly, where their ways come from | chance: 70
- G>W | go back to serve on the crew in your home town, where people need you | odd: leave | v: tradition, benevolence | act: go back to serve on the crew in their home town, where people need them | chance: 85
works: The choice sits right with {N}, and within a season the people around {N} greet {N} as one of their own.
works: One of the regulars, who never quite fit in either, confides something to {N} that the rest of the crew has never heard.
fails: At the next drill {N} fumbles in front of everyone, and the story goes round {place} for weeks.
fails: The jokes stop, but so do the invitations, and {N} is left out of the crew's evenings.

== a newcomer's slip at the big practice
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: BRG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, safety
horizon: months
roles: keeper, regular, colleague
sphere: prot
ladder: newcomer
cast_want: odd
earth: you are the odd one out at a place you go: in front of the whole crew, doing it your own way went wrong
times: A token's mistakes are noticed far more than anyone else's, and people who do not fit a place are more likely to leave it (Kanter 1977; misfit and leaving: Kristof-Brown et al. 2005)
gap: 3-8
scene: At the big practice at {place}, {N} does a task the way {N} always has, not the way the others do it, and it goes wrong in front of everyone. It is a small slip, quickly put right, but it is the newcomer's slip, and it is the one the whole room remembers. That evening someone pins a cartoon of it to the board. The keeper says the crew can still use {N}, if {N} does it their way from now on.
scene W: {N} let the team down in front of everyone and owes them better; the question is whether that means their way or a fair look at both.
scene U: {N} goes over it again and again: was it the method or the hurry? One slip proves little either way.
scene B: A newcomer's mistake is remembered longer than any veteran's. {N} works out what that cartoon will cost in shifts and chances.
scene R: The cartoon burns. {N} wants to tear it down, or laugh at it, or walk out, all at once.
scene G: {N} was taught this by people at home who never slipped. The shame sits heavy, and so does the pull of the old ways.
- W | own up to the slip, then show the crew your method done right, step by step | odd: hold | v: conformity, self-direction | mark: owned up | act: own up to the slip, then show the crew their method done right, step by step | chance: 60
- U | judge the fit wrong, and join a crew that trains the way you do | odd: leave | v: self-direction, security | act: judge the fit wrong, and join a crew that trains the way they do | chance: 78
- B | take up their way at once: the newcomer who fits in gets on | odd: blend | v: achievement, conformity | chance: 80
- R | laugh at the cartoon, sign it, and carry on doing it your way | odd: hold | v: stimulation, self-direction | act: laugh at the cartoon, sign it, and carry on doing it their own way | chance: 65
- G | go back to the crew at home, among people who know your ways | odd: leave | v: tradition, security | act: go back to the crew at home, among people who know their ways | chance: 72
- W>B | hand in notice properly, and take a post where your record is not a joke | odd: leave | v: power, conformity | act: hand in notice properly, and take a post where their record is not a joke | chance: 80
- U>R | study their method until it comes naturally, so drills feel easy again | odd: blend | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 62
- B>G | find the one regular who backs your way, and build a small circle around it | odd: hold | v: power, benevolence | act: find the one regular who backs their way, and build a small circle around it | chance: 60
- R>W | walk out that night, saying a crew that mocks its own is not fit to serve | odd: leave | v: universalism, stimulation | mark: made an enemy | chance: 75
- G>U | learn their way patiently, as an apprentice would, to understand why it works here | odd: blend | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 68
works: Months later the cartoon is gone from the board, and a regular asks {N} to show the new ones how it is done.
works: Someone from {place} admits to {N}, in private, that they made the very same slip once and never told a soul.
fails: The slip becomes a story told at every gathering at {place}, and {N} hears it again and again.
fails: A second small mistake follows the first, and the keeper stops giving {N} the jobs that matter.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 2: production, newcomer

== the running joke about how the new hand works
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: UB
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: colleague, regular, keeper
sphere: prod
ladder: newcomer
cast_want: odd
earth: you are the odd one out at a place you go: at the workplace you joined a year ago, the way you work has become the running joke
times: People who do not fit a workplace are less satisfied and more likely to leave, so places grow more alike over time (misfit and leaving: Kristof-Brown et al. 2005)
gap: 3-8
scene: A year into {place}, {N} still works in a way nobody else there does: a different order, a different pace, care over different things. It has become the running joke. Each morning someone asks, to laughter, whether {N} will be doing it the strange way again today. The work gets done, and done well, but {N} is plainly the odd one out.
scene W: {N} wants to pull fair weight with the others, and wonders whether doing it differently is fair to them, or simply right.
scene U: {N} has thought hard about this way of working and knows why it suits; whether the others ever will is another question.
scene B: The joke is costing {N} standing, and standing is what gets the better jobs and the raise. Something has to give.
scene R: {N} is worn out by the joke and the morning question. Some days it is easy to laugh along; some days it is not.
scene G: {N} learned this work from family, the way it was always done there, and the old ways feel like home in the hands.
