times: Newcomers who stand out are pressed hardest to fit in, while those who fit in first later earn room to differ (Kanter 1977; idiosyncrasy credit: Hollander 1958)
gap: 3-8
scene: After a long shift at {place}, one of the old hands takes {N} aside. It is kindly meant: {N} works well, but differently, and the difference is starting to rub people the wrong way. Do it the way the rest do for a year or two, the old hand says, and then nobody will mind what {N} does. {N} walks home turning it over.
scene W: The advice is fair and kindly given. {N} owes the old hand a real answer, and owes the place some give.
scene U: There is sense in it: earn standing first, then change things. But {N} wonders what a year or two of the wrong way would teach.
scene B: A year of fitting in for a lifetime of say: as deals go, {N} can see its worth, and its price.
scene R: {N} feels the warmth of the advice and the cage in it at once. Being someone else for two years sounds very long.
scene G: The old hand is right that every place has its ways. {N} has ways too, and they came from somewhere.
