gap: 3-8
scene: A year on at {place}, {N} still goes about things differently from everyone else there. People are friendly enough, but the jokes have started, and this week someone asked, half laughing, whether {N} ever means to fit in. The question hangs in the air longer than the laugh.
scene W: {N} feels the pull of belonging properly, with everyone keeping to the same ways. {N} also feels the pull of what {N} was taught is right.
scene U: {N} wants to know why things are done this way here before deciding whether {Ns} own way is any better.
scene B: Standing out has a price. {N} works out what fitting in would buy, and what it would cost.
scene R: The joke stung more than {N} let on. Pretending to be someone else would sting more.
scene G: {Ns} ways came from home and from the people who raised {N}. They are not habits to drop for strangers.
